course: dictionary
lesson: 10686
title: Unproven ideas
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Look at the word in two parts. Un- means not, and proven means shown to be true. So unproven means not shown to be true yet, because there is not enough evidence or proof.
explain: «One unproven idea says foxes use the Earth's magnetism to aim.» The word unproven tells you the idea might be true, but nobody has shown it yet. An unproven idea is not the same as a wrong idea. It is still waiting for evidence.
explain: Scientists test ideas with experiments and check the results. Until the evidence is strong enough, the idea stays unproven. Adverts can make unproven claims too, so ask «how do we know?» before you believe them.
fact: Unproven means not shown to be true; there is not yet enough evidence or proof.
fact: The prefix un- means not, so proven means shown to be true.
fact: An «unproven idea» is an idea that has not yet been tested or shown to be true.
fact: Unproven does not mean false. It means the truth is not yet shown.
fact: Phrases to know: «unproven claim», «unproven idea», and «remains unproven».
exercise: «One unproven idea says foxes use the Earth's magnetism to aim.» What does unproven mean? | Very fast | *Not yet shown to be true | Proven beyond all doubt | Completely false
exercise: Which phrase means a claim with no proof yet? | *Unproven claim | Glowing light | Barely whisper | Ancient castle
exercise: Scientists want evidence before they call an idea true. What should the idea be called until then? | Luminous | Curt | *Unproven | Famished
test: Why might a scientist call an idea unproven? | *Because it has not been tested or shown to be true yet | Because it glows in the dark | Because it is too long to read | Because it has been proven twice
test: Which sentence uses unproven correctly? | The painting was unproven in the gallery. | *The advert made an unproven claim about magic shoes. | She ate an unproven sandwich for lunch. | The bus was unproven and very yellow.
exam: Ben reads an advert: «Our toothpaste makes you run faster, scientists say.» What should Ben ask first? | Is the word new? | Can I buy it cheaper? | *How do we know? | What colour is the tube?
